From: HAOS Radxa ZERO 3W fork
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: Enable Bluetooth on Radxa ZERO 3W

The AIC8800 is a combo part: its WiFi half talks SDIO, its Bluetooth half
talks H4 over uart1.  Mainline enables uart1 and even declares the three
Bluetooth control pins in pinctrl, but never attaches a device to them, so
the Bluetooth side is simply never brought up.

Radxa drive the very same chip from their vendor tree with the ordinary
mainline serdev binding, declaring it as a bcm43438 - the AIC8800 speaks
the same H4 dialect and its firmware is pushed over SDIO by aic8800_bsp,
so no Broadcom firmware download happens.  Mirror that node here; the GPIO
numbers match the pinctrl groups mainline already defines.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w.dts
@@ -84,9 +84,23 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
 &uart1 {
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&uart1m0_xfer &uart1m0_ctsn &uart1m0_rtsn>;
 	uart-has-rtscts;
 	status = "okay";
+
+	bluetooth {
+		compatible = "brcm,bcm43438-bt";
+		clocks = <&rk817 1>;
+		clock-names = "lpo";
+		device-wakeup-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PB4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		host-wakeup-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PB3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		shutdown-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC1 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		max-speed = <1500000>;
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&bt_reg_on_h &bt_wake_host_h &host_wake_bt_h>;
+		vbat-supply = <&vcc3v3_sys>;
+		vddio-supply = <&vcc_1v8>;
+	};
 };
